 *   **Memory Complexity:** Requires a 3x3 window of memory per pixel access.
-*   **Strengths:** It is an efficient edge-detecting operator that is less sensitive to noise than the Roberts Cross operator due to the smoothing effect of its larger kernel. It produces considerably higher output values for similar edges, which can be beneficial for thresholding in opacity function generation [S1]. It satisfies requirements for isotropy, localization, and signal-to-noise characteristics [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** It is computationally more expensive than simpler operators. The smoothing effect can cause natural edges to appear several pixels wide, potentially reducing spatial precision in edge localization [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** Use when noise robustness is prioritized over maximum computational speed or extreme spatial precision. It is particularly suitable for opacity function generation in volume rendering where the goal is to identify data value boundaries rather than precise spatial edge coordinates [S1].
-*   **When Not to Use:** Avoid when real-time performance is critical and noise levels are low (Roberts Cross may be preferred). Avoid when precise, thin edge localization is required without post-processing, as the smoothing effect widens edges [S1].
